/bin/upm from the Micronix 1.6 standalone

upm is "CP/M for Micronix" - the bridge that lets a CP/M program think
it is on a CP/M disk while it runs as a process under Micronix.  It is
the thing that ran our cross-built CP/M hello world, and it is the part
of that story we cannot read, because its source is not in the tree.

This directory is the reconstruction.  Unlike init and man, upm ships
its own symbol table - 222 of them - so the names in upm.ctl are not
guesses.  They are read straight out of the binary.  The question the
names did not answer was whether upm is assembly or Whitesmith's C; the
function signatures do.

    disas -f upm.ctl upm

It is Whitesmith's C.  88 functions open with a CALL to c.ent or
c.ents - the frame helper that is the signature of a compiled function
- so the bulk of it is reconstructed as C.  The rest is not:

    _upmhead _entry _endentr   the crt and the CP/M entry bridge
    _bios _wboot _badbios      the BIOS jump table and its two stubs
    _tint                      the interrupt handler
    _main _cexit _go _null     hand-written for the same reason as crt

and everything else is library - the syscall stubs that say SYS in the
listing, the string/stdio helpers, and the c.* compiler runtime.  Those
are lib/libu and lib/libc and libexec, not upm.

The layout is the odd thing.  The text segment loads at d645 and the
data at 0100, backwards from every other program, because the CP/M TPA
a .com is loaded into lives at the bottom of the address space and
upm's own code sits above it.  disas assumed segments lay text-data-bss
in increasing order, so before 08-2026 it could not disassemble this at
all; the two fixes are in src/tools/disas.c.

The structure, from _main down:

    _main(argc, argv)           _init() to set up the tables, then
                                _load() if a program was named (DIRECT
                                mode) or _ccp() (INTERACTIVE mode)

    _entry / _endentr           the door a CP/M program comes through:
                                save the micronix stack, switch to
                                _stack, run _cpm, switch back

    _cpm(func, arg)             the BDOS dispatcher.  Reads the function
                                number, indexes _cpmtab, jumps to the
                                handler.  >= 40 goes to _null.

    _cpmtab                     40 words: one handler per BDOS function

    _cconin _cconsta _cdirio     console I/O (funcs 1, 11, 6)
    _copen _cclose _cread        file I/O through FCBs (15, 16, 20...)
    _cwrite _cseek _search
    _cmake _delete _rename       directory operations
    _cdma _csize _rrand _wrand   DMA, size, random records
    _clogin _cgetro _cuser ...   the small ones that read/write a global

    _ccp                         the command processor: banner, getline,
                                 the built-ins _dir _type _era _ren
                                 _save _pip, and _system for a "!" line

The reconstruction order that makes sense is the order the symbol table
sorts by address: the FCB layer (_copen down through _cseek) is one
sitting-next-to-another block of C, then the CCP, then the console
handlers.  The data - _fcb, _disktab, _tabs, _ntab, _files, the buffers
- is read out of the .dis as the functions that touch it are named.

upm is in cmd/DIRS now and builds.  The reconstruction links a little
differently from the original: it compiles larger than the Whitesmith
binary, so it does not keep the backwards d645/0100 split.  Everything
links above the TPA at -Ttext=0xa000, leaving a 40k TPA for the .com,
and the CCP sits up with the BDOS instead of down in the clobbered
region.  The .dis and .ctl here remain the source of the truth for the
parts that were read rather than written.
